    tips: <String>["A short visit beside the car can be enough.", "Revisit the same place before choosing somewhere harder.", "Let your dog set the exploration pace."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Choosing a crowded location for the first visit.", "Pulling the dog towards something they avoid.", "Staying until the dog is exhausted."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog will not leave the starting point.", solution: "Stay there, reward calm observation, and leave without forcing progress."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog startles repeatedly.", solution: "Move to a quieter area or end the visit and choose an easier location next time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog refuses food.", solution: "Increase distance from activity and prioritise leaving safely over completing the exercise.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Allow retreat at all times, use secure equipment, and seek qualified force-free or veterinary support if fear is intense, worsening, or sudden."],
    completionDescription: "During three visits to the same quiet new location, the dog explores voluntarily for five minutes and recovers from mild novelty within thirty seconds.",
  ),
  "confidence-consent-based-handling": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "confidence-consent-based-handling",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog remains relaxed and chooses to continue during one brief low-intensity handling exercise.",
    equipment: <String>["Small high-value rewards", "A non-slip mat", "A quiet familiar room", "An optional soft grooming tool"],
    steps: <String>["1. Invite your dog onto the mat and reward a relaxed approach without restraining them.", "2. Move one hand slowly towards a comfortable area such as the shoulder, then pause.", "3. If the dog stays or leans closer, make a one-second touch, mark, reward, and remove your hand.", "4. Pause again so the dog can remain, move closer, or step away before another repetition.", "5. Complete three comfortable touches and finish before increasing duration or body area."],
    tips: <String>["Start with areas the dog already enjoys being touched.", "A pause, turn away, or step off the mat means reduce or stop.", "Separate training from necessary veterinary or emergency handling."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Holding the dog still to complete the repetition.", "Moving directly to paws, ears, or face because those areas need practice.", "Continuing because the dog accepts food despite tense body language."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog moves away immediately.", solution: "Reward approach and hand movement without touching until confidence improves."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes excited and mouths the hand.", solution: "Use slower reward delivery and shorter sessions when arousal is lower."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Handling has caused fear, pain, or defensive behaviour.", solution: "Stop and seek veterinary assessment and qualified force-free support.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Pain can change handling tolerance; avoid restraining a fearful dog and seek professional help where bite risk exists."],
    completionDescription: "The dog voluntarily remains on the mat for three one-second shoulder or chest touches with loose body language and opportunities to move away.",
  ),
  "confidence-recovery-after-surprise": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "confidence-recovery-after-surprise",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can create distance, observe, and return to a chosen calm activity after a mild surprise.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value food rewards", "A quiet retreat area", "One very mild controllable novelty", "A non-slip surface"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin with your dog able to move freely to a familiar retreat area.", "2. Present one very mild novelty at a distance, then remain quiet and allow the dog to choose where to stand.", "3. Reward looking back at you, sniffing the ground, or any relaxed movement away from the novelty.", "4. Remove or reduce the novelty before repeating and allow at least thirty seconds of recovery.", "5. End after one to three calm observations without asking the dog to approach or touch it."],
    tips: <String>["Recovery is a skill even when the dog never investigates.", "Keep surprises mild and controllable during planned practice.", "Use distance before food if the dog is too worried to eat."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Dropping or moving objects suddenly to test the dog.", "Luring the dog closer than they choose.", "Repeating the novelty before full recovery."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog hides or will not eat.", solution: "Remove the novelty, increase distance, and end the session in a familiar safe area."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog barks or rushes towards the object.", solution: "Use a barrier, create distance, and choose a much milder version next time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Startle responses are new, severe, or worsening.", solution: "Arrange veterinary advice and qualified behaviour support to investigate contributing factors.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never deliberately frighten, corner, or flood a dog; sudden sensitivity can be related to pain, illness, or sensory change."],
    completionDescription: "After one mild controlled novelty, the dog can move to a chosen distance and resume relaxed sniffing, eating, or interaction within one minute.",
  ),
  "confidence-generalise-brave-choices": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "confidence-generalise-brave-choices",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can explore or calmly observe one new feature in two different low-pressure environments by choice.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A harness and lead where needed", "A familiar mat", "Two quiet low-risk environments"],
    steps: <String>["1. Select a quiet new setting with an easy exit and begin well away from the unfamiliar feature.", "2. Place the familiar mat or allow sniffing while the dog observes without being prompted closer.", "3. Reward voluntary orientation, relaxed movement, checking in, and choosing to retreat.", "4. Finish after a small success, then repeat with a similar feature in a second setting on another day.", "5. Compare comfort and keep the easier version in regular practice before increasing novelty."],
    tips: <String>["Confidence can look like calm observation, not only approach.", "Use familiar routines to make new places more predictable.", "One short positive visit is better than a long overwhelming outing."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Choosing a busy environment with several new challenges.", "Blocking the retreat path with the lead or your body.", "Measuring success only by touching the new object."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog will not leave the vehicle or entrance.", solution: "Allow observation from the safe starting point and choose an easier location next time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog explores but startles repeatedly.", solution: "Shorten the visit and increase distance from movement or sound."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Fear affects normal life or is worsening.", solution: "Seek veterinary and qualified force-free behaviour support for an individual plan.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Avoid slippery surfaces, traffic, crowds, uncontrolled animals, and any location where the dog cannot safely retreat."],
    completionDescription: "Across two low-pressure environments, the dog voluntarily observes or explores one new feature while retaining loose movement, food interest, and freedom to retreat.",
  ),
  "focus-check-in": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-check-in",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog voluntarily checks in with you several times during a calm session.",
    equipment: <String>["Small food rewards", "A quiet familiar area"],
    steps: <String>["1. Stand or sit quietly with your dog nearby and keep rewards out of sight.", "2. Wait without saying the dog’s name or asking for eye contact.", "3. Mark the moment your dog turns their head or eyes towards you.", "4. Deliver the reward close to you, then look away and reset.", "5. Finish after five voluntary check-ins or one minute, whichever comes first."],
    tips: <String>["Accept a brief glance rather than demanding a stare.", "Keep sessions under a minute at first.", "Practise when your dog is awake but not highly excited."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Repeatedly calling the dog to make them look.", "Holding food near your face as a lure.", "Waiting so long that the dog becomes frustrated."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "No check-in happens.", solution: "Move to a quieter space and mark even a small ear or head turn."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stares only at your pocket.", solution: "Place rewards in a container behind you and pause until attention shifts."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog wanders away.", solution: "Let them take a break and try again later in a smaller safe area.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Keep the exercise short and optional; do not restrain your dog or demand prolonged eye contact."],
    completionDescription: "The dog voluntarily checks in at least five times within two minutes in a quiet familiar area.",
  ),
  "focus-hold-attention": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-hold-attention",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can maintain relaxed attention for three seconds before being rewarded.",
    equipment: <String>["Small food rewards", "A quiet training area", "A timer"],
    steps: <String>["1. Wait for a voluntary check-in and mark it with calm praise.", "2. Count one second before delivering the reward.", "3. On later repetitions, extend the pause to two and then three seconds.", "4. Reward while your dog’s body remains soft and comfortable.", "5. Release with a clear finish cue and invite a sniff break."],
    tips: <String>["Increase duration by only one second at a time.", "Reward blinking and relaxed posture, not rigid staring.", "Mix easy one-second repetitions among longer ones."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Increasing duration on every repetition.", "Holding the reward visibly near your face.", "Continuing after the dog looks tired or frustrated."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog looks away before three seconds.", solution: "Reward at one second again and build in smaller increments."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog jumps for the reward.", solution: "Deliver lower and sooner while all four paws are down."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes very still or worried.", solution: "End the eye-contact exercise and reward easier orientation such as a head turn.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never force eye contact; some dogs find direct staring uncomfortable, so accept relaxed orientation towards you."],
    completionDescription: "The dog maintains relaxed attention for three seconds in four of five attempts in a quiet area.",
  ),
  "focus-around-distractions": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-around-distractions",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can notice a mild distraction and then check back in with you.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A secure lead and harness if outdoors", "One mild planned distraction"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin far enough from a mild distraction that your dog can eat and respond.", "2. Allow your dog to notice the distraction without asking for attention.", "3. Mark and reward the first voluntary look back towards you.", "4. After success, take one step closer or make the distraction slightly more active—not both.", "5. Complete five check-ins, then move away for a relaxing sniff break."],
    tips: <String>["Distance is your easiest difficulty control.", "Reward spontaneous check-ins generously.", "Keep challenging sets shorter than easy practice."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Working so close that the dog cannot disengage.", "Repeating the dog’s name while they are absorbed.", "Expecting continuous attention in a stimulating place."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot take food.", solution: "Increase distance until their body softens and they can eat normally."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Check-ins stop after moving closer.", solution: "Return to the previous successful distance for the rest of the session."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog scans constantly.", solution: "Use a visual barrier or quieter location and reward one brief orientation towards you.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use secure equipment outdoors and move away if your dog cannot disengage; focus work should not involve forced exposure."],
    completionDescription: "The dog voluntarily checks in after noticing a mild distraction in four of five opportunities at a safe working distance.",
  ),
  "focus-disengage-and-reset": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-disengage-and-reset",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can follow you away from a mild distraction and offer a relaxed check-in after a short reset.",
    equipment: <String>["Small high-value rewards", "A quiet open space", "A standard lead and harness if outdoors"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin at a distance where your dog can notice a mild distraction and still eat.", "2. Say a cheerful movement cue once and turn away in a smooth wide arc.", "3. Reward as your dog follows, then scatter two or three pieces of food for slow sniffing.", "4. Wait quietly for a voluntary check-in and reward it before deciding whether to continue.", "5. Repeat no more than four times and finish if recovery becomes slower."],
    tips: <String>["Movement away is part of the training, not a failure.", "Use a broad turn so the lead remains loose.", "Allow enough sniffing time for arousal to fall."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Repeating the dog’s name while moving away.", "Turning only after the dog is already overwhelmed.", "Immediately returning to the same difficult distance."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog will not follow the turn.", solution: "Increase distance sooner and use a more valuable reward beside your leg."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog follows but cannot settle.", solution: "Move to a quieter area and extend the sniffing or rest break."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog reacts intensely to the distraction.", solution: "Prioritise safety and use the reactivity guidance or qualified professional support instead of focus practice.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not use this exercise to keep a distressed dog near a trigger; create real distance and leave when recovery is poor."],
    completionDescription: "Around one mild distraction, the dog follows a smooth turn away and offers a voluntary check-in within fifteen seconds in four of five attempts.",
  ),
  "focus-predictable-patterns": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-predictable-patterns",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can follow a short predictable pattern and remain engaged without continuous verbal prompting.",
    equipment: <String>["Small food rewards", "A quiet walking area", "A harness and standard lead if outdoors"],
    steps: <String>["1. In a quiet area, take three slow steps and deliver one reward beside your leg.", "2. Pause for a voluntary check-in, mark it, then repeat the same three-step pattern.", "3. After three successful cycles, add one mild background distraction at a safe distance.", "4. Keep the rhythm and reward placement unchanged while the environment changes.", "5. End after four calm cycles and give your dog a release to sniff or rest."],
    tips: <String>["Use a pace your dog can comfortably follow.", "Keep the pattern short enough to remember under pressure.", "Predictability should support choice, not hold the dog near something frightening."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Changing the number of steps every repetition.", "Walking too quickly for the dog to stay relaxed.", "Using the pattern after the dog has stopped eating or responding."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog watches the food hand constantly.", solution: "Keep food in the opposite hand and deliver only after the third step."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog loses the pattern outdoors.", solution: "Return to a quieter location and use one-step or two-step cycles."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes more tense with repetition.", solution: "Stop, create distance, and switch to unstructured sniffing or leave the area.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Patterns are not a substitute for distance from fear, pain, or serious reactivity; stop when body language becomes tense."],
    completionDescription: "With one mild background distraction, the dog completes four three-step patterns while keeping the lead loose and checking in between cycles.",
  ),
  "focus-real-world-duration": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "focus-real-world-duration",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can alternate between brief handler engagement and appropriate environmental exploration during a real-world session.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A harness and standard lead", "A familiar outdoor location", "A timer"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a familiar location and plan three one-minute focus blocks with breaks between them.", "2. During each block, reward voluntary check-ins, loose following, and quick responses to one simple cue.", "3. After one minute, release your dog to sniff or observe for at least thirty seconds.", "4. Adjust distance before the next block if responses became slower or body language tightened.", "5. Finish after the best manageable block rather than extending until focus fails."],
    tips: <String>["Useful focus includes orientation and following, not only staring at your face.", "Make breaks predictable and generous.", "Track recovery and response quality rather than total time alone."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Requesting uninterrupted attention for several minutes.", "Adding a busier location before the current one is easy.", "Treating environmental exploration as a training failure."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Focus drops during the second block.", solution: "Shorten blocks to thirty seconds and lengthen the sniff breaks."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog scans the environment continuously.", solution: "Increase distance from activity and begin with a predictable pattern."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog startles or cannot recover.", solution: "End the session and use confidence or reactivity support appropriate to the cause.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Avoid crowded places with no exit and never force sustained focus around something the dog fears."],
    completionDescription: "In a familiar outdoor setting, the dog completes three one-minute blocks with at least three voluntary check-ins per block and relaxed breaks between them.",
  ),
  "house-training-routine": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-routine",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog follows a predictable routine and toilets in the chosen area with prompt reinforcement.",
    equipment: <String>["Lead if the toileting area is not enclosed", "Food rewards", "A simple toileting log", "Enzymatic cleaner"],
    steps: <String>["1. Take your dog to the chosen area after waking, eating, play, and other likely toileting times.", "2. Stand quietly and allow enough lead for sniffing without starting a game.", "3. Mark calmly as toileting finishes, then reward immediately in that location.", "4. Offer a brief sniff or play afterwards so toileting does not always end outdoor time.", "5. Log the time and result to plan the next supervised trip."],
    tips: <String>["Reward within a second or two of finishing.", "Use the same easy-to-reach area initially.", "Supervise closely between scheduled trips."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Punishing accidents or showing the dog the mess.", "Waiting too long between opportunities.", "Rewarding only after returning indoors."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog plays but does not toilet.", solution: "Return indoors under close supervision and try again after ten minutes."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Accidents occur at similar times.", solution: "Schedule the next outing fifteen minutes before the usual time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog toilets very frequently or has a sudden change.", solution: "Contact a veterinarian to rule out urinary, digestive, or pain-related causes.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never punish accidents; clean calmly and seek veterinary support for sudden changes, straining, pain, or unusual frequency."],
    completionDescription: "The dog toilets in the chosen area and receives an immediate reward on at least four of five scheduled opportunities across two days.",
  ),
  "house-training-signal-and-reward": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-signal-and-reward",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog uses a consistent, low-key signal before being taken to the toileting area.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A consistent door or exit point", "An optional accessible bell or mat", "A toileting log"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a simple signal such as standing on a mat or touching an accessible bell.", "2. Prompt the signal immediately before every scheduled toileting trip.", "3. Open the door as soon as the signal occurs and go directly to the toileting area.", "4. Reward after toileting, not merely for repeatedly sounding the signal.", "5. Gradually pause for one second to let your dog offer the signal before prompting."],
    tips: <String>["Keep the signal easy for your dog’s body and size.", "Respond consistently while the skill is new.", "Continue scheduled trips rather than relying on the signal alone."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Choosing a signal the dog cannot perform comfortably.", "Ignoring genuine requests because the timing seems inconvenient.", "Turning the bell into an unrestricted request for outdoor entertainment."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog uses the signal but does not toilet.", solution: "Offer a brief quiet toileting trip, then return indoors without play."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog never signals independently.", solution: "Prompt immediately before the door opens and reward successful toileting consistently."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog signals too late.", solution: "Increase scheduled opportunities while the signal continues developing.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not withhold toileting access to test the signal, and never punish a missed or late request."],
    completionDescription: "Before four of five scheduled toileting trips, the dog offers or follows one prompt for the chosen signal and then toilets in the correct area.",
  ),
  "house-training-reliability": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-reliability",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog remains reliable with modest changes in room access, timing, or location.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "Gates or a safe rest area", "A toileting log", "Enzymatic cleaner"],
    steps: <String>["1. Review the log and identify a reliable interval that has produced no recent accidents.", "2. Add access to one small new area for ten supervised minutes after successful toileting.", "3. Keep scheduled outings and reward in the correct location as usual.", "4. Change only one factor at a time, such as room, interval, weather, or exit door.", "5. If an accident occurs, reduce freedom temporarily and resume the last successful schedule."],
    tips: <String>["Progress by successful days, not by a fixed calendar.", "Maintain more frequent trips during travel or routine changes.", "Treat setbacks as useful scheduling information."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Giving full household access too quickly.", "Changing several routine factors at once.", "Stopping rewards immediately after a few successful days."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Accidents return in a new room.", solution: "Supervise there for shorter periods immediately after toileting."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Bad weather disrupts the routine.", solution: "Use shelter, high-value rewards, and shorter more frequent opportunities."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "A previously reliable dog changes suddenly.", solution: "Arrange veterinary assessment before assuming the dog has forgotten training.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never punish accidents; sudden regression, pain, increased thirst, or altered toileting needs should be discussed with a veterinarian."],
    completionDescription: "The dog completes seven consecutive days without an accident while one planned increase in supervised freedom is introduced.",
  ),
  "house-training-accident-reset": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-accident-reset",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "You can respond calmly to an accident and change the routine to make the next correct toileting opportunity easier.",
    equipment: <String>["Pet-safe enzymatic cleaner", "A toileting schedule or notes app", "A lead for supervised toilet trips", "Food rewards"],
    steps: <String>["1. If you discover an accident, move the dog away calmly without scolding or drawing attention to the area.", "2. Clean thoroughly with a pet-safe enzymatic product according to its instructions.", "3. Record the time, location, recent activity, last toilet trip, and whether the dog was supervised.", "4. Bring the next toilet opportunity earlier and accompany the dog to the correct location.", "5. Reward immediately after correct toileting and review the schedule after three days."],
    tips: <String>["An accident usually means the plan needs adjustment.", "Supervise actively or use a safe confined area when direct supervision is impossible.", "Take young or newly adopted dogs out after waking, eating, play, and excitement."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Punishing the dog after the event.", "Cleaning with a product that leaves odour detectable to the dog.", "Giving more indoor freedom before reliability is established."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Accidents happen at the same time daily.", solution: "Schedule a supervised toilet trip ten to fifteen minutes earlier."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog toilets again shortly after returning indoors.", solution: "Stay outside longer in a boring safe area and reward immediately after completion."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "A previously reliable dog suddenly has accidents.", solution: "Arrange veterinary advice to check urinary, digestive, pain, mobility, or age-related causes.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Sudden accidents, straining, blood, increased thirst, diarrhoea, pain, or inability to toilet need prompt veterinary attention."],
    completionDescription: "After an accident, the owner completes calm cleanup, records the likely gap, and adjusts the next toilet opportunity without punishment on three occasions.",
  ),
  "house-training-clear-outdoor-signal": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-clear-outdoor-signal",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog uses one chosen behaviour near the exit before being taken promptly to the toileting area.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A consistent exit door", "A lead", "An optional floor target or bell used without startling"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a simple signal such as standing on a mat, sitting by the door, or gently touching a target.", "2. Before each scheduled toilet trip, guide or wait for the signal, then open the door immediately.", "3. Walk directly to the toileting area and remain quiet until the dog toilets or the planned opportunity ends.", "4. Reward toileting at the location, not merely activating the signal.", "5. Respond consistently to genuine signals while separating toilet trips from play outings."],
    tips: <String>["Keep the signal easy for puppies, older dogs, and dogs with mobility limits.", "Take the dog out promptly so the connection stays clear.", "Record false alarms and genuine toileting to adjust the schedule."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Using a loud signal that frightens or excites the dog.", "Ignoring signals because the last trip was recent.", "Turning every signal into a long exciting walk."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog signals repeatedly for entertainment.", solution: "Use a short quiet toilet trip, then return indoors if no toileting occurs."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog waits silently and has accidents.", solution: "Return to scheduled trips and reward any movement towards the chosen signal area."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot reach or use the signal comfortably.", solution: "Choose a passive visual signal and discuss mobility or urgency changes with a veterinarian.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not make a dog wait when urgency is possible; medical changes can increase frequency and need veterinary assessment."],
    completionDescription: "Before four of five scheduled toilet trips, the dog performs the chosen signal and then toilets in the correct location during the prompt outing.",
  ),
  "house-training-new-places-and-weather": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "house-training-new-places-and-weather",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can toilet promptly in a second safe location or on a different suitable surface with a familiar routine.",
    equipment: <String>["A lead", "High-value rewards", "Water and weather protection as appropriate", "Cleaning supplies for travel"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a quiet safe second location and visit at a time the dog is normally likely to toilet.", "2. Use the same lead, route cue, waiting routine, and reward timing used at home.", "3. Keep the area small and boring until toileting occurs, then reward and allow a brief exploration.", "4. Practise separately in mild rain, on a new safe surface, or during a small routine change.", "5. Reduce difficulty immediately if the dog becomes worried, overheated, cold, or unable to settle."],
    tips: <String>["Some dogs develop strong surface preferences.", "Bring a familiar scent cue only when hygienic and appropriate.", "Plan more frequent opportunities during travel or schedule changes."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Waiting until an urgent trip to introduce the new location.", "Walking continuously so the dog stays too distracted to toilet.", "Forcing exposure to severe weather or frightening surfaces."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog holds on in the new place.", solution: "Return to a quieter smaller area and try again at the next likely toilet time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Rain or wind prevents toileting.", solution: "Use a sheltered safe spot and build experience during mild conditions first."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Travel causes repeated accidents or distress.", solution: "Increase scheduled breaks and seek veterinary advice if gastrointestinal, urinary, or anxiety signs persist.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Protect paws and body temperature, provide water, and never delay toileting because conditions or travel plans are inconvenient."],
    completionDescription: "In a second safe location or on one new suitable surface, the dog toilets during the familiar routine on three separate planned visits.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-wait-for-reward": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-wait-for-reward",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog pauses for one second while a reward is held safely out of reach.",
    equipment: <String>["Small food rewards", "A quiet familiar room"],
    steps: <String>["1. Hold one reward in a closed hand at chest height while your dog stands or sits comfortably.", "2. Wait silently for your dog to stop nudging or reaching for one moment.", "3. Mark that brief pause and deliver a different reward from your other hand.", "4. Repeat with an open hand held well above nose level, closing it if the dog reaches.", "5. Finish after five easy one-second pauses and give a clear release cue."],
    tips: <String>["Begin with a fraction of a second.", "Let the dog choose their body position.", "Alternate repetitions with free rewards to reduce frustration."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Expecting a long wait immediately.", "Holding food close enough to tease the dog.", "Repeatedly saying “no” instead of marking success."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog paws or mouths the hand.", solution: "Hold the reward farther away and mark the first tiny pause."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog walks away.", solution: "Let them leave and try later with a shorter, easier repetition."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes vocal or frustrated.", solution: "Give a free reward, end the session, and use half-second pauses next time.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Keep waits short and achievable; do not use food deprivation, prolonged teasing, or physical restraint."],
    completionDescription: "The dog pauses calmly for one second before reward delivery in four of five attempts.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-doorways": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-doorways",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog waits briefly as a safe interior door begins to open and moves through after release.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A secure lead if needed", "A quiet interior doorway"],
    steps: <String>["1. Start at an interior doorway with your dog on the safe side and the door closed.", "2. Touch the handle; mark and reward one moment of staying back.", "3. Open the door a few centimetres, closing it gently if the dog moves forward.", "4. When the dog pauses, open fully and give a clear release cue.", "5. Walk through together, reward, and reset for no more than five repetitions."],
    tips: <String>["Practise on an interior door before an external exit.", "Release quickly while the exercise is new.", "Reward behind the threshold to reduce rushing."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Using a dangerous exterior doorway too soon.", "Pushing the dog backwards with the door or body.", "Making the dog wait so long that frustration grows."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog rushes whenever the handle moves.", solution: "Reward calm behaviour near the closed door before touching the handle again."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog squeezes through the gap.", solution: "Use a lead and a smaller door movement in a safer interior location."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog avoids the doorway.", solution: "Stop the pause exercise and allow free comfortable movement through it.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use secure management near exterior doors and never delay urgent toileting access or trap a worried dog at a threshold."],
    completionDescription: "At a quiet interior doorway, the dog pauses for two seconds as it opens and crosses after release in four of five attempts.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-real-world-distractions": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-real-world-distractions",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can pause briefly before two different real-world rewards without excessive frustration.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A secure lead and harness", "Two safe environmental rewards such as sniffing and greeting a familiar adult"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose one safe reward your dog wants mildly, such as approaching a sniffing spot.", "2. Stop far enough away that the lead remains loose and wait for a brief pause or check-in.", "3. Mark and release your dog to the environmental reward for several seconds.", "4. Move away, reset, and repeat before practising with a second low-intensity reward.", "5. End after four successful releases, mixing in easy food rewards."],
    tips: <String>["Use access to the environment as reinforcement.", "Keep pauses shorter around harder distractions.", "Choose rewards that can be offered safely and predictably."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Demanding long stillness beside an exciting distraction.", "Using tight lead pressure as the cue to stop.", "Withholding normal exercise, water, toileting, or social needs."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot pause at the chosen distance.", solution: "Move farther away or choose a less exciting environmental reward."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Frustration increases across repetitions.", solution: "Use immediate releases for easier choices and finish the session early."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog lunges towards the reward.", solution: "Increase distance and use barriers or another safer reward rather than holding the dog in place.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Avoid excessive waiting and frustration; do not use access to essential needs as leverage, and skip greetings when safety or comfort is uncertain."],
    completionDescription: "The dog offers a two-second pause before release to each of two mild everyday rewards in four of five opportunities.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-leave-it": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-leave-it",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog voluntarily moves attention away from a covered low-value item and turns towards you for reinforcement.",
    equipment: <String>["Low-value food", "Higher-value rewards", "A small container or closed hand", "A quiet room"],
    steps: <String>["1. Place low-value food in a closed hand and hold it still at a safe distance.", "2. Wait without repeating a cue until the dog moves their nose or gaze away.", "3. Mark the disengagement and deliver a better reward from the other hand.", "4. Add the leave-it cue just before presenting the closed hand after several successful repetitions.", "5. Progress to food under a covered container while keeping every practice item physically controlled."],
    tips: <String>["Reward away from the item to strengthen movement towards you.", "Use a neutral tone and let the setup teach the choice.", "Keep real hazards managed rather than relying on a cue."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Pulling the hand away as the dog investigates.", "Repeating the cue while the dog continues trying.", "Progressing to uncovered food too quickly."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog paws or bites at the hand.", solution: "Use a container on the floor, increase distance, and reward the smallest glance away."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog disengages only when food is visible.", solution: "Keep the better reward hidden until after the marked choice."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog guards found items.", solution: "Do not practise removal and seek qualified force-free support for safe management.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Leave-it training does not make toxins, medicines, rubbish, or sharp objects safe; prevent access and seek veterinary help after suspected ingestion."],
    completionDescription: "With a covered low-value item present, the dog moves attention away after one cue and turns to the owner in four of five repetitions.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-settle-on-mat": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-settle-on-mat",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can remain relaxed on a mat for a short period while the handler performs a simple nearby activity.",
    equipment: <String>["A comfortable non-slip mat", "Small food rewards", "A safe chew if appropriate", "A timer"],
    steps: <String>["1. Reward your dog for stepping onto the mat and allow any comfortable standing, sitting, or lying position.", "2. Deliver several rewards slowly between the front paws to encourage staying and lowering arousal.", "3. Perform one easy nearby action, such as sitting in a chair, then return and reward relaxed behaviour.", "4. Increase time in five-second increments while adding only one small household movement.", "5. Give a clear release cue and invite the dog off the mat before the session becomes restless."],
    tips: <String>["Relaxed posture matters more than a formal down.", "Use the mat during calm times before relying on it during visitors.", "Provide regular release, toileting, movement, and social breaks."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Returning the dog to the mat physically.", "Increasing duration and household activity together.", "Using the mat as isolation or punishment."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog repeatedly leaves the mat.", solution: "Shorten the interval and reward more frequently before giving a release."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stays but watches food tensely.", solution: "Use slower delivery, fewer visible rewards, and an easier activity."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot settle despite adequate exercise and rest.", solution: "Check discomfort, environment, and routine and seek veterinary advice for sudden changes.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not tether an unsupervised dog to the mat or require staying when the dog needs water, toileting, movement, or retreat."],
    completionDescription: "While the handler completes one simple nearby activity, the dog remains voluntarily relaxed on the mat for thirty seconds in four of five repetitions.",
  ),
  "impulse-control-maintenance-and-release": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "impulse-control-maintenance-and-release",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can pause briefly in several everyday situations and move only after a consistent release cue.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A mat", "A harness and lead for outdoor practice", "Two familiar everyday setups"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose two familiar situations such as a food bowl, doorway, toy, car exit, or mat release.", "2. Ask for a very brief pause and give the same clear release cue before frustration appears.", "3. Reward calm waiting and also reward responding promptly to the release.", "4. Vary duration by only one or two seconds rather than making every repetition harder.", "5. End each set with an easy quick release and provide normal access to movement or the activity."],
    tips: <String>["The release cue is as important as the wait cue.", "Keep waits shorter in exciting or unfamiliar environments.", "Use physical management around roads and vehicle exits regardless of training level."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Increasing duration until the dog breaks position.", "Repeating wait continuously instead of giving a clear release.", "Using long waits to deny normal needs or access."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog moves before the release.", solution: "Shorten the pause and reward at the point where success is still easy."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog remains frozen after release.", solution: "Encourage one step with movement or a tossed reward and practise faster releases."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Waiting creates whining or rising frustration.", solution: "Reduce duration, add movement breaks, and choose a less exciting setup.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use gates, leads, and closed doors around traffic or vehicle exits; trained waiting is not a substitute for physical safety."],
    completionDescription: "Across two familiar daily situations, the dog pauses calmly for three to five seconds and moves after one consistent release cue in four of five attempts.",
  ),
  "jumping-four-paws-down": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-four-paws-down",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog learns that keeping four paws on the floor makes attention and rewards happen.",
    equipment: <String>["Small food rewards", "A quiet room", "A treat pot near the entry"],
    steps: <String>["1. Approach your dog calmly when all four paws are on the floor.", "2. Mark the grounded position before your dog starts to jump.", "3. Deliver the reward low, close to the floor between the front paws.", "4. Step away briefly, then return and reward another grounded greeting.", "5. Add calm stroking for one second before rewarding if your dog remains grounded."],
    tips: <String>["Reward before excitement builds.", "Keep your hands and rewards low.", "Ask household members to use the same routine."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Waiting for the dog to jump before reacting.", "Holding food high above the dog’s head.", "Using kneeing, pushing, shouting, or frightening corrections."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog jumps as you approach.", solution: "Stop farther away, wait for paws down, then toss the reward low."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog jumps after eating.", solution: "Deliver several small rewards low before calmly stepping away."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes more excited with touch.", solution: "Skip stroking and use quiet food delivery until greetings are steadier.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Avoid physical or frightening corrections; use barriers when jumping could knock over a child or vulnerable person."],
    completionDescription: "The dog keeps four paws on the floor during four of five calm approaches by a familiar person.",
  ),
  "jumping-calm-greetings": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-calm-greetings",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog greets a familiar person with four paws down for several seconds.",
    equipment: <String>["Food rewards", "A secure lead or baby gate", "One familiar helper"],
    steps: <String>["1. Position your dog behind a gate or on a loose lead with enough space to move away.", "2. Ask the helper to approach only while four paws remain down.", "3. If jumping starts, the helper quietly pauses or takes one step away.", "4. Mark grounded behaviour and have the helper deliver a reward low.", "5. End the greeting after three calm seconds and give the dog a break."],
    tips: <String>["Brief greetings are easier than long ones.", "Let the helper turn slightly sideways.", "Practise with one calm person before adding variety."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Allowing the helper to keep advancing during jumping.", "Tightening the lead so the dog bounces against it.", "Expecting a long greeting immediately."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot stay grounded as the helper enters.", solution: "Start with the helper farther away or behind a second barrier."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Food increases excitement.", solution: "Use slower delivery of lower-value food or a calm sniff scatter after the greeting."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog barks as well as jumps.", solution: "Reduce the helper’s movement and reward quiet grounded moments from a greater distance.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use barriers and distance to protect guests; never ask a vulnerable person to act as the training helper."],
    completionDescription: "The dog completes four of five three-second greetings with a familiar helper while all four paws remain on the floor.",
  ),
  "jumping-visitors-and-excitement": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-visitors-and-excitement",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can settle into a managed greeting routine when a planned visitor arrives.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A secure lead or gate", "A mat or resting area", "One prepared visitor"],
    steps: <String>["1. Exercise and toilet your dog normally before the planned visit, then prepare the gate and rewards.", "2. Have the visitor enter while your dog remains behind the barrier at a workable distance.", "3. Reward grounded check-ins and allow a sniff break away from the doorway.", "4. Offer a brief greeting only when the dog can keep four paws down; pause immediately if jumping returns.", "5. Guide the dog to a mat or quiet activity and end practice before excitement rises again."],
    tips: <String>["Tell visitors the plan before they arrive.", "A greeting is optional; calm separation is also success.", "Use several short practices instead of one long visit."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Opening the barrier while the dog is highly aroused.", "Letting every visitor use a different response to jumping.", "Treating management as failure rather than good safety."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Door sounds trigger immediate jumping.", solution: "Practise recorded or gentle door sounds separately at low intensity."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog settles behind the gate but jumps when released.", solution: "Keep greetings behind the barrier for now and build calmer repetitions."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Excitement returns after a few minutes.", solution: "Provide a quiet break with a safe chew in another room.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Manage arrivals with barriers and never use kneeing, pushing, shouting, or intimidation; skip direct greetings when anyone could be knocked over."],
    completionDescription: "During three planned arrivals, the dog remains safely managed and completes at least one five-second four-paws-down greeting in two arrivals.",
  ),
  "jumping-station-on-a-mat": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-station-on-a-mat",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog chooses the mat and keeps four paws down while one person moves nearby.",
    equipment: <String>["A non-slip mat", "Small high-value rewards", "A quiet room", "A gate or lead for management if needed"],
    steps: <String>["1. Place the mat away from the doorway and reward any voluntary step onto it.", "2. Deliver several rewards low on the mat while your dog keeps four paws down.", "3. Take one step away, return, and reward the dog for remaining on or returning to the mat.", "4. Add one gentle movement such as opening an internal door or a helper walking past.", "5. Give a release cue and finish before the dog becomes excited enough to leave repeatedly."],
    tips: <String>["The dog may stand, sit, or lie down; four paws on the mat is enough.", "Keep the mat far enough from visitors to reduce pressure.", "Use barriers so mistakes do not turn into repeated jumping."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Placing the mat directly in the doorway.", "Expecting a long stay before the mat has value.", "Only using the mat when visitors arrive."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog leaves as soon as you move.", solution: "Reduce your movement to a weight shift and reward more often on the mat."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes overexcited by the helper.", solution: "Increase distance, use a gate, and let the helper remain still."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog avoids the mat.", solution: "Try a different non-slip surface and reward approaches without luring or forcing.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use gates or distance when jumping could knock over a child, older person, or anyone with limited balance."],
    completionDescription: "With one person moving calmly nearby, the dog remains with four paws on the mat for ten seconds in four of five repetitions.",
  ),
  "jumping-greetings-with-movement": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-greetings-with-movement",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog keeps four paws down while a familiar helper adds small realistic greeting movements.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A familiar adult helper", "A harness and lead or gate", "A clear retreat path"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin with the dog behind a gate or on a loose lead several metres from the helper.", "2. Reward four paws down while the helper takes one slow step and then stops.", "3. If the dog remains grounded, let the helper turn sideways, speak softly, or take another step.", "4. Pause or have the helper move away immediately if jumping begins, then restart with less movement.", "5. Complete three calm approaches and finish before adding touch or prolonged interaction."],
    tips: <String>["Helpers should keep hands low and avoid leaning over the dog.", "Reward before excitement peaks.", "Use movement away as a normal reset, not a punishment."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Adding walking, talking, and touching in the same repetition.", "Allowing the helper to push the dog down.", "Continuing after repeated jumping without reducing difficulty."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog jumps when the helper speaks.", solution: "Return to silent movement and reward calm responses before adding one quiet word."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stays down but becomes tense.", solution: "Increase distance and allow the dog to opt out rather than requiring a greeting."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog redirects onto the lead or handler.", solution: "End the setup, create space, and seek qualified support if arousal is difficult to manage safely.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use adult helpers who can follow the plan and do not practise with vulnerable people until reliability is strong."],
    completionDescription: "During three planned approaches, the dog keeps four paws down while a familiar helper walks two steps and speaks briefly at a safe distance.",
  ),
  "jumping-maintenance-in-public": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "jumping-maintenance-in-public",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can complete brief chosen greetings in two familiar public settings without repeated jumping.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A well-fitted harness and lead", "A helper who follows instructions", "Space to decline or end greetings"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a quiet public location and one familiar helper rather than inviting greetings from strangers.", "2. Ask the helper to approach sideways and stop before the dog becomes highly excited.", "3. Reward four paws down, allow one or two seconds of greeting, then call the dog back for another reward.", "4. End the greeting while it is successful and move away for a sniffing break.", "5. Repeat in a second familiar setting on another day before gradually varying helpers."],
    tips: <String>["It is acceptable to decline greetings completely.", "Keep greetings shorter than the dog’s current excitement limit.", "Use a visible hand signal or clear words so helpers know when to stop."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Letting every passer-by greet the dog.", "Waiting for jumping before calling the dog away.", "Using unfamiliar crowded places as the first generalisation step."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog jumps during the first approach.", solution: "Increase starting distance and practise only the approach without contact."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "People ignore the greeting plan.", solution: "Step away, decline the greeting, and choose trained helpers instead."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog appears worried rather than excited.", solution: "Stop greeting practice and use confidence-building with greater choice and distance.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never require greetings; protect the dog and public by declining contact whenever excitement, fear, or injury risk is present."],
    completionDescription: "Across two familiar low-traffic locations, the dog completes three brief helper greetings with four paws down and returns to the handler when called.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-reward-zone": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-reward-zone",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog takes several relaxed steps beside you while the lead stays loose.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness or flat collar", "A standard lead", "Small food rewards"],
    steps: <String>["1. Fit the equipment comfortably and begin in a quiet room or yard.", "2. Choose the side where you want rewards to appear and deliver three treats beside that leg.", "3. Take one step; mark and reward if the lead remains loose.", "4. Build to three or four steps, rewarding in the same zone.", "5. Pause for sniffing or a short break before another brief set."],
    tips: <String>["Keep your reward hand close to your leg.", "Walk at a comfortable pace for your dog.", "Use sniff breaks as an additional reward."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Holding the lead continuously tight.", "Rewarding out in front where the dog learns to forge.", "Taking too many steps before rewarding."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog rushes ahead.", solution: "Start beside a wall, reward after one step, and build more slowly."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog bites the lead.", solution: "Pause movement, offer a food scatter, and resume when the lead is still."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog lags behind.", solution: "Use a lighter pace and reward any voluntary movement beside you.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use secure, correctly fitted equipment and reward-based handling; avoid painful equipment and sharp lead corrections."],
    completionDescription: "The dog walks five consecutive steps in the reward zone with a loose lead in four of five short attempts indoors or in a quiet yard.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-direction-changes": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-direction-changes",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog follows gentle turns while keeping slack in the lead.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness or flat collar", "A standard lead", "Food rewards"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin walking in a quiet open area and reward two loose-lead steps.", "2. Say a consistent phrase such as “this way” before slowing down.", "3. Turn in a broad curve rather than pivoting sharply.", "4. Mark when your dog turns with you and reward beside your leg.", "5. Practise left, right, and gentle U-turns with sniff breaks between sets."],
    tips: <String>["Signal before every direction change.", "Keep turns broad enough for your dog to balance.", "Reward check-ins as well as position."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Turning without warning and tightening the lead.", "Drilling rapid turns until the dog is dizzy or frustrated.", "Expecting perfect heel position."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog reaches the end of the lead.", solution: "Make the cue earlier and begin the turn before the lead tightens."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stops during turns.", solution: "Use a larger curve and reward the first step that follows you."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog loses interest.", solution: "Add a sniff break after two successful turns and shorten the session.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Check that the walking surface is safe and never use a turn to jerk or pull the dog off balance."],
    completionDescription: "The dog follows four of five signalled direction changes in a quiet area without the lead becoming tight.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-real-world-distractions": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-real-world-distractions",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog maintains a mostly loose lead on a short route with planned distractions.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness", "A standard lead", "High-value rewards", "A quiet planned route"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a short route at a quiet time and begin before your dog becomes highly excited.", "2. Reward several easy steps before approaching a mild distraction.", "3. Create more space or change direction as soon as the lead begins to tighten.", "4. Reward check-ins and loose-lead movement past the distraction.", "5. Finish with a sniff in a safe area and note which distance worked."],
    tips: <String>["Plan escape routes before starting.", "Pay more often in harder sections.", "Count overall loose-lead stretches rather than expecting perfection."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Starting on the busiest possible route.", "Continuing forward while the dog pulls hard.", "Using painful equipment or sharp corrections."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog pulls from the doorway.", solution: "Practise the first ten metres at a quieter time and scatter food before leaving."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "A surprise distraction appears.", solution: "Turn away calmly, add distance, and reward once your dog can respond."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Walking deteriorates late in the route.", solution: "Shorten the route and finish before fatigue or over-arousal builds.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use secure equipment, avoid traffic hazards, and choose distance rather than force when a distraction is too difficult."],
    completionDescription: "Across a five-minute planned walk, the dog completes at least four separate ten-step stretches on a loose lead around mild distractions.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-stop-and-reset": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-stop-and-reset",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog learns that a loose lead restarts movement while a tight lead leads to a brief calm pause and easier setup.",
    equipment: <String>["A well-fitted harness", "A sturdy standard lead", "Small food rewards", "A quiet walking area"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin in a quiet area and reward two or three steps with the lead hanging loosely.", "2. When the lead becomes tight, stop smoothly without pulling the dog backwards.", "3. Wait for any small movement that creates slack, then mark and reward beside your leg.", "4. Resume in an easier direction or take only one step before rewarding again.", "5. End after several successful resets rather than trying to complete a long route."],
    tips: <String>["A reset can include turning away from the distraction.", "Keep your lead hand steady and close to your centre.", "Reward before the lead reaches full tension whenever possible."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Holding constant pressure on the lead.", "Waiting for a perfect heel position before moving again.", "Trying to outlast a dog who is too excited to learn."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog keeps leaning into the harness.", solution: "Turn and move a few steps away from the attraction, then reward the first loose step."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Stopping causes frustration or barking.", solution: "Use shorter pauses and increase distance from the exciting area."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The walk contains almost no loose steps.", solution: "Practise indoors, in a driveway, or in a quiet car park before returning to the route.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Use a secure well-fitted harness and avoid roadside practice until the dog can respond reliably in quieter places."],
    completionDescription: "During a five-minute quiet walk, the dog creates slack and resumes with the handler after a calm stop in four of five pulling moments.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-sniffing-rewards": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-sniffing-rewards",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can walk a short loose-lead section and then access a chosen sniffing spot as the reward.",
    equipment: <String>["A well-fitted harness", "A standard lead", "A quiet route with safe sniffing areas", "Food rewards for backup"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a visible safe sniffing spot and begin several metres away where your dog can still respond.", "2. Reward one or two loose-lead steps towards the spot and pause if the lead tightens.", "3. After another short loose stretch, give a clear release cue and walk with the dog to the sniffing area.", "4. Allow fifteen to thirty seconds of relaxed sniffing without repeatedly cueing attention.", "5. Move away calmly and repeat with a new spot, keeping the walking requirement achievable."],
    tips: <String>["Use smells your dog genuinely values.", "Keep the release cue different from the walking cue.", "Some walks can be mostly sniffing with only brief training sections."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Using sniffing only after a long period of perfect walking.", "Pulling the dog away before they have had time to investigate.", "Choosing a spot beside traffic, rubbish, or another unsafe hazard."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog lunges towards the sniffing spot.", solution: "Start farther away and reward one loose step before moving diagonally towards it."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog will not leave the spot.", solution: "Offer a food scatter away from it and choose a shorter sniffing period next time."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Sniffing makes arousal rise rather than fall.", solution: "Choose a quieter location and use slower exploratory sniffing instead of highly exciting scent.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Check sniffing areas for toxic substances, sharp objects, wildlife, and traffic before giving access."],
    completionDescription: "The dog completes three separate loose-lead approaches of at least five steps and waits for the release cue before reaching a safe sniffing spot.",
  ),
  "loose-lead-longer-routes": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "loose-lead-longer-routes",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can complete a longer familiar route with repeated relaxed sections, planned sniff breaks, and calm resets.",
    equipment: <String>["A well-fitted harness", "A standard lead", "Food rewards", "Water in warm conditions", "A familiar low-risk route"],
    steps: <String>["1. Divide a familiar route into three short training sections separated by sniffing or rest breaks.", "2. Begin with frequent rewards in the first section and use calm resets before pulling becomes sustained.", "3. Give a planned sniff break, then restart with an easy direction change and a reward beside your leg.", "4. Shorten or skip the final section if arousal, fatigue, heat, or pulling increases.", "5. Record which section was hardest and begin the next walk with an easier plan for that location."],
    tips: <String>["Distance is less important than the percentage of relaxed walking.", "Vary routes only after familiar ones are manageable.", "Use management rather than training through crowded bottlenecks."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Adding distance and distractions at the same time.", "Withholding breaks until the dog has walked perfectly.", "Continuing through fatigue because the planned route is not finished."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Pulling increases near the end of the route.", solution: "Reduce the total distance and finish after the second successful section."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "A particular corner always causes pulling.", solution: "Cross earlier, increase distance, or use a different route while training that location separately."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog becomes unable to take food or respond.", solution: "Leave the area, provide recovery, and choose a much quieter route next time.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Avoid heat stress, unsafe surfaces, traffic risk, and crowded areas where distance cannot be created."],
    completionDescription: "Across a familiar fifteen-minute route, the dog completes three relaxed loose-lead sections with planned breaks and recovers after brief tension without lead corrections.",
  ),
  "reactivity-safe-distance": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-safe-distance",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "You can identify and maintain a distance where your dog notices a trigger without reacting intensely.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness", "A sturdy standard lead", "High-value food rewards", "A spacious location with an easy exit"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a spacious location where triggers are predictable and can be observed from far away.", "2. Check that the harness and lead are secure, then position yourself near a clear exit route.", "3. When a trigger appears, watch whether your dog can eat, respond to their name, and keep a relatively soft body.", "4. Mark and reward calm noticing without moving closer to the trigger.", "5. Increase distance immediately if your dog stiffens, cannot eat, cannot disengage, or struggles to recover."],
    tips: <String>["Begin farther away than you think you need.", "Use parked cars or vegetation as visual barriers.", "Keep sessions brief and include decompression afterwards."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Moving closer to test the dog’s limit.", "Allowing forced greetings.", "Holding the lead tight while remaining near the trigger."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog reacts as soon as the trigger appears.", solution: "Choose a quieter location, add a visual barrier, or work at a much greater distance."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot take food.", solution: "Leave the area calmly; inability to eat is a clear sign the setup is too difficult."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Reactions are intense, unpredictable, or worsening.", solution: "Pause app exercises and contact a qualified force-free trainer or veterinary behaviour professional.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never deliberately provoke a reaction or risk a greeting. Three app lessons cannot resolve every serious reactivity or aggression case; seek professional help when there is injury risk, and veterinary support for sudden changes that may involve pain or illness."],
    completionDescription: "Across three trigger sightings, the dog remains able to eat, respond, and recover within thirty seconds at the recorded safe distance.",
  ),
  "reactivity-look-and-disengage": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-look-and-disengage",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can notice a trigger and voluntarily turn away at a safe distance.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness", "A sturdy standard lead", "High-value food rewards", "A location with generous distance and exits"],
    steps: <String>["1. Start beyond the safe distance recorded in the foundation lesson.", "2. Let your dog notice the trigger briefly without asking them to approach.", "3. Mark the first head turn, eye movement, sniff, or body shift away from the trigger.", "4. Deliver several rewards while moving another step away.", "5. End after three successful disengagements and leave before the trigger becomes harder."],
    tips: <String>["Reward tiny disengagements at first.", "Movement away can be part of the reward.", "Use only predictable triggers with ample space."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Waiting for full eye contact before rewarding.", "Reducing distance after every success.", "Continuing when the dog cannot recover between sightings."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stares without disengaging.", solution: "Increase distance or move behind a visual barrier rather than repeating cues."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog takes food roughly.", solution: "Treat that as rising arousal, move farther away, and end the set early."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog redirects towards the lead or owner.", solution: "Prioritise immediate distance and obtain qualified professional support before further setups.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Keep the dog below threshold, avoid forced greetings, and never place any person or animal at risk. Serious cases need individual force-free and veterinary support beyond these three app lessons."],
    completionDescription: "At a safe distance, the dog voluntarily disengages from a visible trigger within five seconds in four of five opportunities.",
  ),
  "reactivity-controlled-exposure": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-controlled-exposure",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can complete a short controlled session with repeated recovery and no forced interaction.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness", "A sturdy standard lead", "High-value food rewards", "A skilled adult helper if appropriate", "Barriers and an exit plan"],
    steps: <String>["1. Plan the trigger type, starting distance, barriers, helper movement, and exit before bringing your dog into view.", "2. Begin beyond the dog’s established working distance and confirm they can eat and respond.", "3. Present one brief, predictable trigger movement while reinforcing looking and disengaging.", "4. Move away for at least thirty seconds of recovery before another presentation.", "5. Complete no more than three successful presentations and finish while the dog remains settled."],
    tips: <String>["Use a qualified force-free professional to plan setups when risk is uncertain.", "Change only distance, duration, or movement in one session.", "A calm retreat is a successful training choice."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Using uncontrolled public encounters as practice.", "Asking the helper to approach for a greeting.", "Continuing because the dog managed one difficult repetition."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Recovery takes longer after each presentation.", solution: "End the session and make the next setup shorter and farther away."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot eat, respond, or disengage.", solution: "Increase distance immediately and leave using the planned exit."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Behaviour presents an injury risk.", solution: "Do not continue independently; work with a qualified force-free trainer or veterinary behaviour professional.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["No app setup should risk injury or use flooding. Three lessons cannot resolve every serious reactivity or aggression case; seek veterinary support for sudden changes and professional help for intense, worsening, or unpredictable reactions."],
    completionDescription: "In a planned below-threshold setup, the dog completes three brief trigger presentations while eating, disengaging, and recovering within thirty seconds each time.",
  ),
  "reactivity-emergency-u-turn": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-emergency-u-turn",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can follow a familiar turn cue away from a mild distraction before a reaction develops.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure well-fitted harness", "A sturdy standard lead", "High-value food rewards", "A quiet open practice area"],
    steps: <String>["1. Practise with no trigger present by saying the turn cue and moving in a smooth wide arc.", "2. Reward beside your leg as the dog catches up, then scatter food after several steps away.", "3. Repeat until the dog begins turning promptly without lead pressure.", "4. Add one mild distant distraction and use the cue before body language becomes tense.", "5. Move to a safe location or behind a barrier and allow full recovery before deciding what to do next."],
    tips: <String>["Keep the cue cheerful and distinct from everyday conversation.", "Practise in both directions.", "A successful exit is more important than completing the planned walk."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Waiting until the dog is already lunging or unable to eat.", "Turning sharply and pulling the dog around.", "Using the cue to circle back towards the trigger immediately."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog does not turn with you.", solution: "Practise again without a trigger and reward after only one following step."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog turns but looks back repeatedly.", solution: "Continue increasing distance and use a visual barrier before offering a sniff scatter."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "An unexpected trigger is already too close.", solution: "Prioritise physical safety, use barriers or parked cars, and leave without attempting a training repetition.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["This is an exit skill, not a way to keep approaching triggers; use secure equipment and professional support where injury risk exists."],
    completionDescription: "With a mild distant distraction present, the dog follows the turn cue and moves at least five metres away on a loose lead in four of five attempts.",
  ),
  "reactivity-recovery-after-trigger": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-recovery-after-trigger",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can move to safety and return to eating, sniffing, or responding before the session ends.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure harness and lead", "High-value food rewards", "A quiet recovery location", "A simple recovery-time log"],
    steps: <String>["1. After a trigger event, use the practised exit and move behind a barrier or well beyond the previous distance.", "2. Stop cueing and allow the dog time to orient, breathe, and look around safely.", "3. Offer a small food scatter or familiar easy cue only when the dog can engage without pressure.", "4. Record how long it takes for eating, sniffing, loose movement, or normal responsiveness to return.", "5. End the session or choose a much quieter route rather than presenting the trigger again."],
    tips: <String>["Recovery can take minutes or longer after a difficult event.", "Use the dog’s behaviour, not the clock alone, to judge readiness.", "Plan quiet days after unusually stressful experiences."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Asking for sustained eye contact immediately after a reaction.", "Returning to the trigger as soon as the dog takes one treat.", "Interpreting refusal of food as stubbornness."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot eat or sniff after substantial distance.", solution: "Leave the environment and provide quiet recovery at home or in a safe vehicle."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Recovery time is increasing across sessions.", solution: "Reduce exposure frequency and difficulty and seek professional guidance."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog redirects aggression or creates injury risk.", solution: "Stop independent setups and work with a veterinary behaviour professional or qualified force-free trainer.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not place hands near a highly aroused dog’s collar or face; use distance, barriers, and professional safety planning."],
    completionDescription: "After one mild unexpected trigger, the dog reaches a safe recovery area and returns to relaxed eating, sniffing, or simple responsiveness before the session ends.",
  ),
  "reactivity-generalisation-and-maintenance": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "reactivity-generalisation-and-maintenance",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can use established coping skills around one known trigger type in two carefully controlled locations.",
    equipment: <String>["A secure harness and lead", "High-value rewards", "Barriers and exit plans", "A qualified helper or professional when appropriate", "A training log"],
    steps: <String>["1. Select a second low-risk location with more space than you expect to need and plan an immediate exit.", "2. Begin farther from the known trigger type than the successful distance used in the first location.", "3. Reinforce noticing, disengaging, and voluntary movement away during no more than three brief sightings.", "4. Provide full recovery between sightings and stop if response quality or recovery worsens.", "5. Compare both locations and keep practising at the easier one while slowly building familiarity with the second."],
    tips: <String>["Treat each new location as a new level.", "Change only one factor such as distance, movement, or location at a time.", "Maintenance may always include management and avoiding close encounters."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Assuming success in one park transfers automatically everywhere.", "Using uncontrolled dogs or strangers as helpers.", "Reducing distance because the dog stayed quiet once."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The second location is much harder.", solution: "Increase distance, shorten the session, or return to the original location before trying again."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Triggers cannot be controlled reliably.", solution: "Do not use that location for training; choose a predictable setup with safe exits."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Progress has stalled or reactions are worsening.", solution: "Seek veterinary review and qualified individual behaviour support before adding challenge.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["No app plan can make serious aggression risk safe on its own; use professional assessment, secure management, and avoid forced exposure."],
    completionDescription: "Across two controlled locations, the dog completes brief below-threshold sightings of one known trigger type while disengaging and recovering without forced contact.",
  ),
  "recall-name-response": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-name-response",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "foundation",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog turns towards you promptly after hearing their name once.",
    equipment: <String>["Small high-value food rewards", "A quiet familiar room"],
    steps: <String>["1. Stand close to your dog in a quiet room with rewards ready.", "2. Wait until your dog is looking elsewhere without being deeply distracted.", "3. Say their name once in a warm, clear voice.", "4. Mark the instant they turn towards you, then deliver a reward beside your leg.", "5. Pause for a few seconds and repeat five times, ending while your dog is keen."],
    tips: <String>["Use the same pronunciation each time.", "Reward even a small head turn at first.", "Practise in different quiet rooms before adding distractions."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Repeating the name several times.", "Using the name when something more exciting is too close.", "Calling the dog only when fun is about to end."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Your dog does not turn.", solution: "Move closer, reduce background activity, and make one soft sound before the next repetition."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Your dog watches the food instead.", solution: "Keep rewards behind your back until after the head turn."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Responses become slower.", solution: "Stop for a break and use fewer repetitions next time.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Practise indoors or in a securely enclosed area so there is no need to chase your dog."],
    completionDescription: "The dog turns towards the owner after one name cue in at least four of five attempts from one metre away in a quiet room.",
  ),
  "recall-short-distance": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-short-distance",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog moves directly to you after one recall cue from three metres away.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value food rewards", "A secure enclosed space", "A lightweight long line if outdoors"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin in a secure, familiar area with your dog two metres away.", "2. Say the dog’s name, then give your recall cue once as they look towards you.", "3. Move backwards two steps to invite movement without pulling the dog.", "4. Mark as the dog reaches you and reward generously close to your body.", "5. Release the dog back to exploring, then repeat from gradually varied positions."],
    tips: <String>["Use especially good rewards for recall.", "Sometimes recall and release straight back to fun.", "Increase distance only after four reliable responses."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Showing the reward before giving the cue every time.", "Reeling the dog in with the long line.", "Punishing or scolding the dog after they return slowly."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog stops halfway.", solution: "Shorten the distance and move away cheerfully as the dog starts towards you."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog ignores the cue outdoors.", solution: "Return indoors or increase distance from outdoor distractions."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog avoids being touched on return.", solution: "Reward first and practise gentle collar touches separately.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Never punish a dog after they return; use a long line outdoors and keep it attached to a secure harness."],
    completionDescription: "The dog returns after one recall cue in at least four of five attempts from three metres away in a familiar, low-distraction area.",
  ),
  "recall-around-distractions": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-around-distractions",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog can leave a mild distraction and return on cue while safely managed.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A 5–10 metre long line", "A secure well-fitted harness", "A mild planned distraction"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a spacious area and begin far enough from one mild distraction that your dog can still respond.", "2. Allow your dog to notice the distraction without moving closer to it.", "3. Say their name and recall cue once when they can briefly disengage.", "4. Move away, mark the turn towards you, and reward several times when they arrive.", "5. Release them to sniff, changing only one factor—distance or distraction—after repeated success."],
    tips: <String>["Treat returning as worthwhile every time.", "Use natural rewards such as permission to sniff.", "Record the distance where your dog succeeds."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Testing recall beside an overwhelming distraction.", "Shortening the long line with a sharp pull.", "Moving to off-lead practice before it is reliably safe."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog cannot look away.", solution: "Increase distance immediately and wait for relaxed body language before trying again."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Success drops below four in five.", solution: "Return to the last easier distance and use a better reward."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog tangles in the line.", solution: "Use an open area, keep the line low, and gather slack smoothly without wrapping it around your hand.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not practise off lead in unsecured areas until recall is reliably safe; a long line is a safety tool, not a correction tool."],
    completionDescription: "On a long line, the dog returns in four of five attempts from five metres while one mild distraction remains at a workable distance.",
  ),
  "recall-reward-reset": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-reward-reset",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "life-skills",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog begins turning and moving towards you promptly because the recall cue reliably predicts a worthwhile outcome.",
    equipment: <String>["Five to ten pea-sized high-value rewards", "A quiet secure room or enclosed yard", "One lower-value reward for comparison"],
    steps: <String>["1. Begin in a secure quiet area and wait until your dog is only mildly occupied.", "2. Say the name and recall cue once, then move backwards two steps without showing food.", "3. Mark the first turn towards you and deliver three small high-value rewards close to your body.", "4. Release your dog back to the previous safe activity so returning does not always end the fun.", "5. Repeat four times, then finish with an easy response and a brief play or sniff break."],
    tips: <String>["Vary rewards between food, play, praise, and permission to explore.", "Keep rewards hidden until the dog commits to returning.", "Use fewer, better repetitions instead of testing the cue repeatedly."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Repeating the cue after the first attempt.", "Using recall mainly to end enjoyable activities.", "Increasing distance before enthusiasm has returned."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog waits to see food first.", solution: "Keep food out of sight, shorten the distance, and reward the smallest prompt turn."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog approaches but stays out of reach.", solution: "Reward before touching, then practise collar handling in the separate arrival lesson."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The response becomes slower during the set.", solution: "Stop immediately, provide a break, and use only three repetitions next time.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Practise only in a secure area or on a long line attached to a well-fitted harness; never punish a slow return."],
    completionDescription: "Without seeing food first, the dog turns and moves towards the owner after one cue in four of five attempts from two metres in a quiet secure area.",
  ),
  "recall-collar-touch-and-release": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-collar-touch-and-release",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog returns all the way to you and stays relaxed while you briefly touch or hold the collar before rewarding and releasing.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value food rewards", "A secure enclosed area", "A comfortable flat collar or harness"],
    steps: <String>["1. With your dog beside you, gently touch the collar or harness, mark, reward, and release.", "2. Repeat until the dog remains loose and comfortable, then take one light hold for one second.", "3. Ask for a recall from one to two metres and reward the arrival before reaching towards the collar.", "4. Touch or hold the collar briefly, deliver another reward, then give a clear release cue.", "5. Gradually vary your body position while keeping the distance short and the handling gentle."],
    tips: <String>["Reach from the side rather than over the dog’s head.", "Sometimes reward and release without attaching the lead.", "Watch for leaning away, lowered posture, or lip licking and make the touch easier."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Grabbing quickly as soon as the dog arrives.", "Holding the collar longer after every repetition.", "Using the exercise only when play is about to stop."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog backs away from the hand.", solution: "Return to rewarding a hand movement near the shoulder without making contact."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog mouths the hand or becomes overexcited.", solution: "Use slower food delivery, pause between repetitions, and practise when arousal is lower."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog has a history of fear or defensive behaviour around handling.", solution: "Stop direct practice and seek qualified force-free professional support for an individual plan.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Do not restrain or corner a worried dog; handling should remain brief, optional, and comfortable."],
    completionDescription: "After a short recall, the dog approaches within reach and remains relaxed for a one-second collar or harness touch in four of five repetitions.",
  ),
  "recall-real-world-maintenance": LessonContent(
    lessonId: "recall-real-world-maintenance",
    contentVersion: 1,
    category: "behaviour",
    minimumDogAgeMonths: null,
    goal: "Your dog maintains a strong recall response across familiar locations without being repeatedly tested beyond their current ability.",
    equipment: <String>["High-value rewards", "A 5–10 metre long line", "A secure well-fitted harness", "A simple recall practice log"],
    steps: <String>["1. Choose a familiar outdoor area and identify the distance and distractions your dog can currently manage.", "2. Complete two easy recalls before attempting one slightly more challenging but still safe repetition.", "3. Reward every return generously and release the dog back to sniffing when it is safe to do so.", "4. Record location, distance, distraction, and success so the next session begins at an achievable level.", "5. Finish with an easy recall, then leave before fatigue or environmental pressure reduces reliability."],
    tips: <String>["Keep using a long line whenever the environment is not securely enclosed.", "Refresh the cue with easy indoor games each week.", "Treat an ignored cue as information to reduce difficulty, not disobedience."],
    commonMistakes: <String>["Removing the long line after one successful session.", "Calling repeatedly when the dog is already over threshold or deeply engaged.", "Reducing rewards because the dog seems trained."],
    troubleshooting: <LessonTroubleshooting>[LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "Reliability varies greatly between locations.", solution: "Train each location as a new level and begin with shorter distance and fewer distractions."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The dog returns slowly after a difficult distraction.", solution: "Reward the return, then increase distance from that distraction for future practice."), LessonTroubleshooting(problem: "The cue has been ignored many times.", solution: "Pause use of the cue outside training and rebuild it with the reward-reset lesson in easy settings.")],
    safetyNotes: <String>["Recall is never a guarantee around traffic, wildlife, livestock, or other serious hazards; use secure physical management."],
    completionDescription: "On a long line in two familiar outdoor locations, the dog returns after one cue in at least four of five planned attempts while remaining relaxed and safe.",
  ),
};

LessonContent lessonContentById(String lessonId) {
  final content = productionLessonContent[lessonId];
  if (content == null) {
    throw StateError('Missing production lesson content for $lessonId.');
  }
  return content;
}

String lessonImageAssetPath(String lessonId) =>
    'assets/lesson-images/by-lesson/$lessonId.jpg';
